                <pb facs="0688" n="681" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg681"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">124.93/376: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-11-15T15:00:00+08:00">November 15, 1937—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 15—9:05 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>917. Your 353, November 14, 4 [<hi rendition="#italic">3</hi>] p.m. British
                    Chargé d’Affaires is due from Shanghai in a day or two and will consult and
                    report when they have arrived. Hitherto its [<hi rendition="#italic">his?</hi>]
                    plan was to go to Shanghai sending a subordinate, perhaps Military Attaché, with
                    Chinese Government. French associates intend to remain Shanghai, subordinate
                    secretary representatives now here to follow Government. German Ambassador only
                    other Ambassador in Nanking will go as far as Hankow with us. It is not clear
                    here as to what Government may do or when it will do it.</p>
                <p>Department’s 353 received in such garbled condition that I shall have to reply
                    specifically later when repeats requested have been received. But above
                    represents information available after discussion which I had with other
                    representatives this morning.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
